-            <p>Members \(1, \dots, k\), with \(k \ge 2\), trade in a cycle: member \(t\) owes member \(t+1\) the amount \(a > 0\) for \(t < k\), and member \(k\) owes member 1 the amount \(a\). Show that every net obligation is zero, that \(A = 0\), and that the reduction is 10000 basis points.</p>
+            <p>Members \(1, \dots, k\), with \(k \ge 2\), trade in a cycle: member \(i\) owes member \(i+1\) the amount \(a > 0\) for \(i < k\), and member \(k\) owes member 1 the amount \(a\). Show that every net obligation is zero, that \(A = 0\), and that the reduction is 10000 basis points.</p>
             <details class="solution"><summary>Solution</summary>
               <p>Each member receives \(a\) from its predecessor and pays \(a\) to its successor, so \(\mathrm{Rec}_i = \mathrm{Pay}_i = a\) and \(n_i = 0\) for every \(i\). Then \(A = \sum_i \max(0,0) = 0\). The gross notional is \(G = ka > 0\), so the reduction is \(ka\) cents and \(\lfloor 10000 \cdot ka/(ka) \rfloor = 10000\) basis points. For \(k = 2\) the cycle is two trades, one in each direction, which cancel completely.</p>
             </details>
